#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
guestpe -- compile and link a real x86-64 Windows PE DLL from C sources.

Every other guest-facing module in this tree ends up on the guest side as a
spec2thunk trap stub: an AMD64 PE whose every export is three instructions
that trap back into the host, where ntdll's dispatcher marshals MS-x64 to
ELFv2 and calls the native ppc64 implementation.  steamclient64.dll cannot be
served that way -- see dlls/lsteamclient/steamclient_guest.h for the reasons
-- so it is real compiled machine code the guest CPU (well: the guest
instruction stream FEX executes) actually runs.  This tool is the whole
"compiler" for that: given a small declarative source list (a .guestpe
file -- see dlls/lsteamclient/steamclient64.guestpe for the annotated
original and its keyword reference), it runs clang once per source with the
x86_64-windows-gnu target and links the result against the guest thunk DLLs
of whatever this module imports.

WHY A SEPARATE TOOL AND NOT MORE make RULES.  tools/makedep.c's
output_source_guestpe() emits ONE rule for the whole DLL, exactly as it does
for a .thunks file: make's job is dependency tracking (does steamclient64.dll
need to be relinked), not orchestrating 47 separate compiler invocations with
their own object-file rules threaded through Wine's already-elaborate
per-architecture SOURCES machinery, none of which this module uses (there is
no ppc64 half, no IMPORTS=, no multiarch object_files[] arrays -- see
output_source_guestpe's own comment for exactly what make DOES track: the
.guestpe file, this script, the .def file, every explicit SOURCE line, and
the guest thunk DLLs named by IMPORT lines).

Everything this script needs to know beyond "which files" -- the target
triple, the include paths, the warning flags, the entry point convention --
is the fixed, hand-verified recipe recorded in the task that produced this
file; it is NOT reconstructed from Wine's normal winegcc/winebuild flags,
because none of those apply to a module with no native half.

